Given the parametric equation of curve $C_1$ as $$\begin{cases} x=2-t \\ y= \sqrt {3}t \end{cases}, (t\text{ is the parameter})$$, when $t=-1$, it corresponds to a point $A$ on curve $C_1$, and the symmetric point of $A$ with respect to the origin is $B$. Establishing a polar coordinate system with the origin as the pole and the positive half-axis of $x$ as the polar axis, the polar equation of curve $C_2$ is $$\rho= \frac {6}{ \sqrt {9+3\sin^{2}\theta}}$$.
