If there exists $1\leqslant i\leqslant n$ and $1\leqslant j\leqslant n$ such that $a_{i,j}$ is both the maximum value in the $i$-th row and the minimum value in the $j$-th column, then the table $A$ is called an "$N$-table" and $a_{i,j}$ is called an "$N$-value" of the table $A$.  
For any given $n$, the set of all "$N$-tables" is denoted as $\Omega_{n}$.  
(1) Determine whether the following tables are "$N-(2)$ tables". If yes, write down one of its "$N-(3)$ values"; $A= \begin{Bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\ 7 & 8 & 9\end{Bmatrix}$, $B= \begin{Bmatrix} 1 & 4 & 7 \\ 8 & 2 & 5 \\ 6 & 9 & 3\end{Bmatrix}$;  
(II) Prove: If table $A$ is an "$N$-table", then its "$N$-value" is unique;  
(III) Randomly select a table $A$ from $\Omega_{19}$, denote the "$N$-value" of $A$ as $X$, and calculate the mathematical expectation $E(X)$ of $X$.

