$(2) xy$.

In the Cartesian coordinate system $xoy$, the curve $C_1$ is defined by the parametric equations $\begin{cases} x=t\cos \alpha, \\ y=t\sin \alpha, \end{cases}$ (with $t$ as the parameter, $t\neq 0$), where $0 \leqslant \alpha < \pi$. In the polar coordinate system with the origin $O$ as the pole and the positive $x$-axis as the polar axis, the curves $C_2$ and $C_3$ are defined by $\rho =2\sin \theta$ and $\rho =2\sqrt{3}\cos \theta$, respectively.

(Ⅰ) Find the Cartesian coordinates of the intersection points of $C_2$ and $C_1$;

(Ⅱ) If $C_2$ intersects $C_1$ at point $A$, and $C_3$ intersects $C_1$ at point $B$, find the maximum value of $|AB|$.

Let $g$ be defined by \[g(x) = \left\{
